### 21. Servers and Network Use.

To run a covered work "as a server" means to run it, or let it be run, so
that other people connect to it, or play, communicate or exchange data
through it, over a computer network. This includes dedicated, lobby,
matchmaking and relay servers, game servers that a community runs, services
that replace or stand in for an online service of a covered work or of the
game it modifies, and cloud gaming and other services that run a covered
work for players who do not control the computer it runs on.

Normal play is not running a covered work as a server, even where the
previous paragraph would include it. Normal play means taking part in a
session, a lobby or a match, as a player or as a spectator, from a copy of
the game that you play on a computer that you use, whether you host it or
join it and whatever network connects the players. The peer-to-peer play
that a covered work provides, over Steam or any other network, is normal
play. Playing a covered work yourself on a computer that you rent or reach
remotely, with no one else playing through that copy, is also normal play.
A person who hosts stays in normal play while they pause, step away for a
short time, wait in a menu or a lobby, or watch after their character is
out of play. A copy is run as a server when it hosts more than one session,
lobby or match at once, or when it is run to host sessions, lobbies or
matches for other people while no one takes part in them from it: for
example a copy left running with no one at it, a copy started in a headless
or dedicated mode, one of several copies that one person runs to host other
people, or a copy in which the one who takes part as host is a program,
script or bot, or a person who is there only to keep it open. Showing your
own play in a video or stream is not running a server.

You may not run a covered work as a server without the Licensor's prior
written permission. Anyone may ask the Licensor for it, through the contact
that the Licensor names at the publication address named in section 0. A
permission may be given to one person or organization, or to everyone, or
to everyone who meets the terms it states, by publishing it at the
publication address or in the NOTICE file of a release. A permission covers
only the works, servers, period and terms that it names, does not pass to
anyone else unless it says so, and gives no right to an Excluded Party. A
permission ends when its holder becomes an Excluded Party or acts for one,
and it does not cover a server that advertises, links to, or sends players,
accounts or data to, an Excluded Service. Section 20 and the next paragraph
apply to a server run under a permission.

If you modify a covered work, and other people can interact with your
modified version through a computer network, as players, through a server
or in any other way, you must offer each of them the Corresponding Source of
your modified version, at no charge. You meet this duty for everyone who
uses a copy of your modified version if that version shows a prominent
notice, for example on its about, credits, lobby or connection screen, that
gives a working address where anyone can copy that Corresponding Source from
a network server, at no charge, without registration, membership or
personal data other than a free account on a public code host, by a
standard or customary means of copying software, and if you keep that
address working for as long as you offer that version or let other people
interact with it. Giving a person that Corresponding Source, or a written
offer of it, under section 6 also meets this duty for that person. If you
run as a server, under a permission given under this section, a modified
version that someone else made, you have the same duty toward the people
who connect to that server, and you meet it by keeping that version's
notice while its address works, or by making the offer yourself. A player
who, in normal play, runs a modified version that someone else made,
without changing it, has no duty under this paragraph, but must not remove
or hide its notice. Changing a setting or a key binding, or translating the
text that a covered work shows its users, is not modifying it for this
paragraph. This paragraph applies notwithstanding any other provision of
this License, including the sentence of section 0 under which interaction
through a computer network is not conveying. You need not make the offer to
an Excluded Party (section 13).

This section does not bind the Licensor. A breach of this section is a
violation of this License. Section 8 applies to it as to propagating a
covered work otherwise than this License expressly provides. Reinstatement
under section 8 applies only from the time of reinstatement. It does not
make lawful what was done in breach before it.

